# Line endings.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, so a checkout
# rewrites text files to CRLF. That is invisible until one of them is copied to
# a server and run: a shell script with CRLF line endings fails on the first
# line with "\r: command not found", which reads as a corrupt file rather than a
# checkout artefact. The two kinds of file here that are executed or parsed on a
# Linux host are pinned to LF.
#
# Deliberately NOT "* text=auto": that would renormalise every existing file in
# the repository on the next commit and bury a real change in the diff.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf

# The same hazard, in files with no extension to match on. An rc script is a
# shell script whatever it is called, and everything under the OPNsense plugin's
# files/ tree is read on a FreeBSD firewall - the rc script, the configd
# template targets, the PHP, the XML and the volt view. rc.netbsd was already
# exposed to this: the index holds LF, so pinning these changes nothing in the
# repository and only stops a Windows checkout handing a BSD a file whose first
# line fails with ": command not found".
packaging/bsd/rc.*         text eol=lf
packaging/opnsense/files/** text eol=lf

# Same hazard again, for the init templates and the two new packagers. None of
# these has an extension for the rules above to match on, and all of them are
# executed or sourced on a non-Windows host:
#
#   deploy/init/*        installed as an init script (runit's run, an s6 run,
#                        /etc/init.d, a launchd plist)
#   xbps INSTALL/REMOVE  copied INTO the .xbps and run by xbps on Void
#   packaging/portage/** ebuilds are bash sourced by portage; breeze-core.confd
#                        is sourced by OpenRC, where a trailing CR would become
#                        part of BREEZE_HOST
#
# A CRLF checkout of any of them fails on its first line with
# "\r: command not found", or binds a host address with a carriage return in
# it, and reads as a corrupt file rather than a checkout artefact.
deploy/init/**          text eol=lf
packaging/xbps/INSTALL  text eol=lf
packaging/xbps/REMOVE   text eol=lf
packaging/portage/**    text eol=lf

# GitHub parses these itself, and a CRLF checkout of a YAML issue form is the
# kind of thing that fails in the form editor rather than anywhere useful.
.github/**              text eol=lf
